From cab3e9925a1d274686cf53c1776d4b20e31af329 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 18:58:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/21] LibJS+LibWasm+LibGfx+AK: Fix 32-bit and big-endian host
 assumptions

32-bit hosts:
- PropertyKey kept an 8-byte union (u64 numeric keys) while asserting
  it is pointer-sized. Keep the 8-byte storage everywhere, route all
  whole-key copies through it, and on 32-bit hosts put the tag and the
  low index bits in the word that m_bits aliases (the high word on
  big-endian hosts) with the remaining index bits in the other word.
- Shape's exact size assertion only holds with 64-bit pointers; use an
  upper bound on 32-bit hosts.
- LibWasm converted Module pointers to and from u64 with bit_cast, which
  requires equal sizes; go through FlatPtr.
- The WASI flag overlays used bool bit-fields; on ABIs with a 4-byte
  bool (Darwin PowerPC) those are allocated in 4-byte units and change
  the overlay size, so use u8 bit-fields.

Big-endian hosts:
- WebAssembly linear memory is little-endian, but the store path relied
  on a LittleEndian<> round trip that cancels itself out for integers
  (and would not even compile for v128). Make ConvertToRaw produce the
  host bit pattern and serialize once in store_value(); assemble v128
  loads from two little-endian halves.
- AK::LittleEndian/BigEndian converted floating-point values
  numerically instead of by bit pattern, and could not handle 16-byte
  integers (LittleEndian<u128> is used by the Wasm parser and LibDNS).
  Swap floats as their bits and multi-word integers per word.
- DataView/TypedArray raw byte conversion reversed bytes based on the
  requested order alone; compare it with the host order instead, and
  never "reverse" a single byte.
- The Matroska float reader overlaid a float on the high half of a u64
  union; take the low bits explicitly.
- The ICO directory structures are little-endian on disk; read them
  through LittleEndian<> instead of plain integers.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
